    {
      "type": "teach",
      "title": "Welcome to Chapter 3",
      "body": "You can read every letter and build simple sentences. This chapter takes you to real Shavian text — first the endings that hang off ordinary words, then the schwa that fills them, then the everyday words English has been misspelling for centuries, then dialogue. After that you stop practising and simply read: two stories, and a genuine published fable to finish. Everything is still spelled by sound; you just haven’t met all its habits yet."
    },
    {
      "type": "teach",
      "title": "Plurals follow your voice",
      "body": "Say “cats”, then “dogs”. The s buzzes in dogs but not in cats — so Shavian writes cats 𐑒𐑨𐑑𐑕 and dogs 𐑛𐑪𐑜𐑟. There is no one-size “-s” to memorize: write the sound you actually make."
